%% Answer plans for every past T5 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T5QA.

**2017 Q5 — Status of women's health; improvement within available resources.** Define health (WHO 1946); health mirrors status. Maternal (MMR 186, PMMS 2019; skilled attendance about two-thirds; rural, Balochistan). Nutrition (anaemia 42%; stunting 40%; NNS 2018). Reproductive (TFR 3.6; contraception 34%; early marriage, age 16). Other burdens (breast cancer, cervical cancer and HPV 2025; mental health; violence). Causes tree (son preference, mobility, decision power, poverty, spending about 1% of GDP, few female staff). Programmes (LHWs 1994, midwives, Sehat Sahulat, BISP, Nashonuma). Principle: prevention and primary care before hospitals. Low-cost measures (LHWs, family planning, iron and folic acid, fortification, skilled attendance, referral, telemedicine) — mapping figure. Social measures (marriage age, men and mothers-in-law, health literacy). Better use of money (BISP targeting, partnerships, accountability). Most deaths preventable at low cost.

**2018 Q5 — Why second-worst despite conventions and the Constitution.** 143rd of 144 (2017): promises vs practice. Index and four sub-indices. Commitments: Arts 25, 34, 35, 37(e), 51; CEDAW 1996, Beijing 1995, SDGs 2015. Gaps: economic (LFPR, pay, informality), education (literacy 53/68, girls out of school), health (MMR, anaemia, son preference), political (reserved seats, few ministers). Reasons: enforcement gap; patriarchal norms (purdah, honour, early marriage); poverty and low spending; mobility, safety and unpaid care; weak political will, CEDAW declaration, federal–provincial gaps after 2010, crises. Cycle figure. South Asian comparisons (Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, Nepal). Way forward (women's work, enforcement, girls' secondary schools, women in cabinet); 2026: 143/145 shows movement is possible.

**2019 Q6 — Paid employment and reproductive roles: challenges worldwide and in Pakistan.** Double burden. Terms: reproductive role, Moser's triple role, second shift (Hochschild 1989). Trend: rising worldwide; Pakistan 21.4% → 24.4%. Worldwide: time poverty (women do about 3× the unpaid care — ILO 2018); pay gap and comparable worth; segregation, glass ceiling, sticky floor; motherhood penalty, childcare, maternity protection; harassment and informal work. Cycle figure. Pakistan: mobility and transport; norms (breadwinner, honour); joint families; informality (agriculture, home-based); harassment; scarce childcare; provincial gaps (Punjab 33.8%, Balochistan 14.5%). Benefits (income, voice — Kabeer 2000). Laws (Harassment Act 2010/2022; Maternity and Paternity Leave Act 2020; Sindh HBW Act 2018; 10% quota). Share care; make work decent.

**2020 Q6 — A system that guarantees rights, enables enjoyment and gives speedy remedy.** Human rights as the base of a democratic welfare state; statement echoes the NCHR Act 2012. Pyramid: guarantee → enjoyment → remedy. Guarantee: Arts 8–28 (enforceable), 25, 25A, 34, 35; CEDAW 1996, ICCPR 2010, ICESCR 2008; laws (2006, 2010, 2011, 2016, 2021); institutions (NCHR, NCSW 2000/2012, ombudspersons, provincial commissions). Enjoyment: low literacy and awareness, poverty, custom (honour, inheritance denial, early marriage). Remedy: police refusal and delay, slow courts, low convictions, *jirgas*, under-funded commissions and shelters. Cycle figure. Critical evaluation: strong on paper, weak in practice; progress (2016 honour law after Qandeel Baloch; 2021 special courts; 2025 ICT marriage age). Way forward: legal literacy, policing, courts, one-stop centres, strong commissions.

**2023 Q7 — Current health status; measures for women's status as a whole.** Define health and status. Health picture: maternal (186; two-thirds skilled attendance), nutrition (42%; 40%), reproductive (3.6; 34%; 241 million growing 2.55%), cancers (HPV 2025), mental health, violence, floods (650,000 pregnant women). Causes (norms, poverty, health system, literacy). Measures with examples: health (LHWs, midwives, telemedicine); education (Punjab stipend 2004; Art. 25A); economic (Sindh HBW Act 2018; Leave Act 2020; transport, childcare); legal (2021 special courts; Sindh 2013 and ICT 2025 marriage age; 2020 property law); political and social (women ministers — none in 2025; Women's Parliamentary Caucus 2008; BISP 2008; engaging men). Cycle figure. Dimensions rise together.

**2024 Q2 — Fathalla's "maternity death road"; health in social, cultural and demographic context.** Fathalla, Nairobi 1987, Safe Motherhood Initiative, "Why did Mrs X die?"; societies have yet to decide women's lives are worth saving. Road (born female and poor → anaemia → no school → early marriage → many pregnancies → no antenatal care → complication → delays → death) — flow figure. Three delays (Thaddeus and Maine 1994). Social (son preference, anaemia 42%, poverty, literacy 53%, decision power). Cultural (marriage age 16; 18 in Sindh 2013, ICT 2025; purdah; female-doctor preference; home births and *dais*; taboos). Demographic (241 million; 2.55%; TFR 3.6; 34%; young; rural; Balochistan; 2022 floods). Mapping figure. A Pakistani Mrs X. Wider issues; programmes; closing the road at every stage.

**2026 Q4 — Pakistan in the Global Gender Gap Report 2025; factors behind the ranking.** 148/148, 56.7% (2025); 143/145, 59.5% (2026). Index since 2006; gaps not well-being; global average about 69%. Evaluation by sub-index: economic (weakest; 144th in 2026); education (53/68; WEF 48.5%; 25m+ out of school; 130th in 2026); health (MMR 186; anaemia; sex ratio); political (zero women ministers; 11 women won general seats in 2024; Maryam Nawaz). Grid figure. Socio-economic factors: LFPR 24.4% (Punjab 33.8%, Balochistan 14.5%; informal; pay gap); norms, mobility, unpaid care, poverty; spending (health about 1%, education about 2% of GDP). Political factors: weak will and representation; enforcement gap (2010, 2016, 2021 laws); 18th Amendment; crises (2022 floods, IMF austerity). Cycle figure. 2026 rise (education and politics up 11 places; economics unchanged). Way forward; paid, decent work is the test.

**2026 Q7 — Intersectionality and marginalised women: health, education, employment.** Averages hide the worst-off (Lahore banker vs Tharparkar labourer). Crenshaw 1989; Collins 1990 matrix of domination. Systems: patriarchy, class, place, religion and caste, ethnicity, ableism — Venn figure. Rural women (distance, unpaid farm work, no land, *jirgas*, *vani/swara*). Religious minorities (forced conversion and marriage of Hindu and Christian girls; scheduled-caste bonded labour; Christian sanitation workers; Hindu Marriage Acts 2016/2017). Ethnic minorities (Hazara widows; Balochistan; former tribal areas merged 2018). Disabled women (stigma; inaccessible clinics and schools; 1981 quota; CRPD 2011; ICT Act 2020). Table: group × health, education, employment. Single-axis policy fails. Policy for the overlaps (mobile clinics, accessible schools, protection against forced conversion, social security, CNIC and marriage-registration drives, disability quotas, disaggregated data, voice).

**2026 Q8(a) — Short note: digital transformation and the mobile gender gap.** (~555 words.) Gap in access, skills, benefits; GSMA; South Asia widest; 38% = 62 women per 100 men. Progress 38% → 25%; eight million more women online (2024–25); widest for rural, poor, older, less-educated; shared handsets. Drivers: cheaper phones and data; digital BISP payments; CNICs; skills programmes; social media and freelancing; network effect. Barriers: cost; CNIC for a SIM; literacy 53%; norms and male control; online harassment. Compare figure: opportunities (home-based income, mobile wallets, telemedicine, learning, voice) vs risks (harassment, surveillance, insecure platform work, new divide). PECA 2016; Digital Rights Foundation helpline 2016. Way forward; SDG 5.b; access plus skills and safety.
